sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch01-07> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Domestic races differ like congeneric species, but usually less" ;
    dct:description "Apart from their lesser uniformity, their often monstrous development of one part, and their fertility when crossed, domestic races of a species differ from each other in the same manner as closely allied species of a genus in nature, though in most cases to a lesser degree." ;
    delphi:context "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter I, “Variation under Domestication”; section “Character of Domestic Varieties”. Scope: domestic races compared with closely allied wild congeneric species" ;
    dct:type "empirical-generalization" .
}

